1
00:00:05,586 --> 00:00:25,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Ask about dealing with insults, or annoying people, or just in general, how to not be offended by what others are doing, or what they're saying to you, no matter what, how insulting, how much they're insulting you.

2
00:00:26,151 --> 00:00:30,092
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you become Buddha-proof?

3
00:00:30,112 --> 00:00:30,532
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

4
00:00:32,613 --> 00:00:33,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that is, that is a...

5
00:00:36,544 --> 00:00:43,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Another thing that... that has to be rooted in... in restraint beforehand.

6
00:00:46,031 --> 00:00:53,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, because it's... you get offended by people, by insults, because your mind is... has no control over itself.

7
00:00:54,467 --> 00:01:06,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, immediately, it jumps at the worst scenario, jumps at the insult, and starts not wanting it, resisting it, refusing it, want to retaliate because of it.

8
00:01:07,431 --> 00:01:13,874
[SPEAKER_00]: But if the mind was... if you were able to keep your mind in check, then the insult would just come and go, and you wouldn't be affected by it.

9
00:01:14,474 --> 00:01:20,836
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't expect to be able to keep your mind in check, if your body hasn't been kept in check, and your speech hasn't been kept in check.

10
00:01:22,826 --> 00:01:29,652
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why... that's why it's... it's a hard thing to do, in a way, because people are generally impatient.

11
00:01:30,192 --> 00:01:40,161
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they have a problem, they want to fix, but you tell them, well, in order to fix it, you need to start building this basis for it, for the... for the fix, for the solution.

12
00:01:40,521 --> 00:01:50,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you're a... if you're a well-restrained person in regard to your own desires, that usually move people left and right, if you... if you have... if you have confidence, if you have

13
00:01:50,670 --> 00:01:54,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Confidence comes from the restraint, from being able to say no to yourself.

14
00:01:55,153 --> 00:02:02,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, yeah, you wouldn't lose perspective even when others basically abuse you verbally or even physically.

15
00:02:03,338 --> 00:02:07,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you wouldn't... the mind would not feel beaten because of it.

16
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:09,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's... it's basically the mind gives in.

17
00:02:10,943 --> 00:02:14,045
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind makes itself insulted and affected by it.

18
00:02:15,810 --> 00:02:17,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Because those... the words...

19
00:02:17,611 --> 00:02:20,833
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the physical actions, they stop at your body.

20
00:02:20,953 --> 00:02:22,714
[SPEAKER_00]: The words, they stop at a level of speech.

21
00:02:22,754 --> 00:02:29,178
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, it's the mind that picks it up, resists it, doesn't want it, hates it, gives in to your will, on account of it, or similar.

22
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:55,342
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if someone calls you an idiot, your views are ridiculous.

23
00:02:55,982 --> 00:02:59,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you were... Any reason you're going to get offended?

24
00:03:00,524 --> 00:03:05,967
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you get offended if you were not... if you were not clear about your own views beforehand.

25
00:03:06,167 --> 00:03:17,392
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were holding on to them for various unwholesome reasons, such as, you know, sense of identity or because you fancy yourself as an intelligent person, smart person, intellectual person,

26
00:03:19,256 --> 00:03:29,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Or just generally insecure, and then you kind of find solace in conviction and faith, and then you solve all the challenges that you did, like, struck right in your core.

27
00:03:30,579 --> 00:03:35,300
[SPEAKER_00]: But if your views abuse the product of restraint and wisdom, nobody can tell it.

28
00:03:35,320 --> 00:03:37,100
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the whole world can disagree with it.

29
00:03:37,860 --> 00:03:41,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you wouldn't be able to be affected by it, because there's nothing wrong with it.

30
00:03:42,021 --> 00:03:43,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not based on wholesome.

31
00:03:44,022 --> 00:03:44,982
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't harm anyone.

32
00:03:45,542 --> 00:03:45,702
[SPEAKER_00]: And,

33
00:03:46,995 --> 00:03:48,956
[SPEAKER_00]: It frees you from suffering right there and now.

34
00:03:49,416 --> 00:03:51,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, right here and now.

35
00:03:52,457 --> 00:04:01,501
[SPEAKER_00]: In the sense of that you have a direct verification that you're not pulled and controlled by your own desires anymore, as much as you used to be.

36
00:04:02,322 --> 00:04:04,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means there's already that sense of confidence.

37
00:04:04,162 --> 00:04:05,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, no, this works.

38
00:04:05,263 --> 00:04:05,743
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

39
00:04:05,963 --> 00:04:07,644
[SPEAKER_00]: People can come and tell you, no, that doesn't work.

40
00:04:08,124 --> 00:04:08,904
[SPEAKER_00]: That's ridiculous.

41
00:04:08,924 --> 00:04:09,865
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just nonsense.

42
00:04:10,462 --> 00:04:12,963
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't make a difference to you, because actually you've seen it work.

43
00:04:13,823 --> 00:04:16,603
[SPEAKER_00]: So you would only... the only conclusion would be, ah, they don't know what you're talking about.

44
00:04:16,643 --> 00:04:18,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if they were to know, they would agree with this.

45
00:04:19,304 --> 00:04:20,404
[SPEAKER_00]: It's universally right.

46
00:04:22,405 --> 00:04:25,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Freedom from desire, in that sense, yeah, means... means freedom.

47
00:04:29,046 --> 00:04:36,127
[SPEAKER_00]: So if you... if you have that kind of clarity, internally, then insults, well, won't be able to insult you.

48
00:04:36,648 --> 00:04:38,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Like if somebody says something in ill-will,

49
00:04:40,145 --> 00:04:43,306
[SPEAKER_00]: His ill-will, or her's ill-will, wouldn't necessarily cause your ill-will.

50
00:04:43,867 --> 00:04:47,348
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you wouldn't, because you're not full of ill-will, you wouldn't lose your own perspective.

51
00:04:47,808 --> 00:04:53,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, you would be able, actually, to hear what they're saying, even if it's a complete ridiculous nonsense insult.

52
00:04:54,211 --> 00:04:58,732
[SPEAKER_00]: You would still be able to hear it, because you're not immediately, automatically on a defensive.

53
00:04:59,573 --> 00:05:02,454
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, sometimes, you would hear, actually, he has a point there.

54
00:05:02,694 --> 00:05:06,456
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, he's completely wrong, because he's full of ill-will, and coming from a wrong place.

55
00:05:06,676 --> 00:05:07,576
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not my problem.

56
00:05:08,216 --> 00:05:08,336
[SPEAKER_00]: But

57
00:05:10,456 --> 00:05:13,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I should have known better.

58
00:05:13,118 --> 00:05:13,759
[SPEAKER_00]: I made a mistake.

59
00:05:14,159 --> 00:05:15,220
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

60
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:16,962
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be able to take it personally.

61
00:05:19,944 --> 00:05:22,907
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be able to... Jabu, come here.

62
00:05:23,848 --> 00:05:25,449
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be able to take it personally.

63
00:05:27,911 --> 00:05:29,032
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the problem.

64
00:05:29,312 --> 00:05:35,598
[SPEAKER_00]: People cannot help but take this... Well, people's mind cannot help but take whatever comes their way personally.

65
00:05:36,095 --> 00:05:44,297
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, immediately end up either rejoicing in praise, or defending against the blame, and attacking, and fearing blame as well.

66
00:05:44,797 --> 00:05:51,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's completely groundless and ridiculous, they'll still fear just the phenomenon of blame aimed at them.

67
00:05:52,878 --> 00:05:59,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter by whom, what for, if it's complete, like if it's just utter fabrication, there's no base or truth.

68
00:05:59,439 --> 00:06:03,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter, they'll still try to avoid blame for the sake of it, because they fear it.

69
00:06:04,058 --> 00:06:10,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they don't have that clarity and transparency and control over their own mind, to not be able to jump in it, in that irrational manner.

70
00:06:19,149 --> 00:06:21,851
[SPEAKER_01]: What about things like, eh, that annoy you?

71
00:06:22,131 --> 00:06:30,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, eh, I suppose, like the weather, or... Well, again, it's the same, same, similar attitude, just slightly more, eh, slightly more subtle.

72
00:06:32,766 --> 00:06:33,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you pull the curtain, please?

73
00:06:34,327 --> 00:06:37,848
[SPEAKER_00]: My coat is filled with mosquitoes.

74
00:06:37,909 --> 00:06:43,791
[SPEAKER_00]: The... annoyance... Again, it is a form of you resisting things that are not really in your control.

75
00:06:44,332 --> 00:06:45,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you gave the example of the weather.

76
00:06:46,093 --> 00:06:47,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, why are you annoyed?

77
00:06:47,453 --> 00:06:50,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have desires that require a good weather.

78
00:06:51,835 --> 00:06:57,118
[SPEAKER_00]: And while those desires are present, the bad weather is causing that discrepancy that's making you annoyed.

79
00:06:57,138 --> 00:06:58,058
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to get rid of it.

80
00:06:58,859 --> 00:07:01,120
[SPEAKER_00]: But, can you get rid of the bad weather?

81
00:07:01,862 --> 00:07:02,203
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

82
00:07:02,263 --> 00:07:06,789
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's important to recognize that that discrepancy is causing you to suffer.

83
00:07:07,269 --> 00:07:13,137
[SPEAKER_00]: If it is, then you will recognize that the way that you can remove that suffering is remove the discrepancy.

84
00:07:13,758 --> 00:07:18,684
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you can't change the better, you can't change the external circumstances,

85
00:07:19,285 --> 00:07:25,090
[SPEAKER_00]: In the nature, which means what you can change is change what you want at the time, change your desire.

86
00:07:25,530 --> 00:07:33,937
[SPEAKER_00]: If you remove the desire that was requiring a nice sunny day, you wouldn't be affected by it, by no amount of rain or any other bad weather.

87
00:07:33,977 --> 00:07:38,681
[SPEAKER_00]: It just wouldn't affect you.

88
00:07:39,382 --> 00:07:45,787
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were just... if you had a desire to just stay and not go anywhere, just... it doesn't matter if you don't.

89
00:07:47,458 --> 00:07:49,379
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're annoyed, there's got to be a reason.

90
00:07:52,241 --> 00:07:55,303
[SPEAKER_00]: And that reason is that... that basically what makes that discrepancy.

91
00:07:55,323 --> 00:08:06,350
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can either continue blaming the circumstances, blame the weather, blame the world, blame the other people, or recognize that that is inevitable and that's not where that suffering is rooted, nor that discrepancy.

92
00:08:07,231 --> 00:08:10,693
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in things that you can say no to, which is your own desire.

93
00:08:12,634 --> 00:08:15,436
[SPEAKER_00]: But people take that for granted, so they never want to say no to their own desire.

94
00:08:15,825 --> 00:08:17,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why it's always someone else's fault.

95
00:08:17,906 --> 00:08:20,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever suffering arises, it's because of this or because of that.

96
00:08:21,327 --> 00:08:27,910
[SPEAKER_00]: It's never because, oh, I crave and I cause discrepancy with my desires, and as a result of that discrepancy, I suffer.

97
00:08:28,030 --> 00:08:30,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Once again.

98
00:08:30,552 --> 00:08:31,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire once again.

99
00:08:32,072 --> 00:08:33,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving once again.

100
00:08:33,313 --> 00:08:33,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

101
00:08:39,148 --> 00:08:44,770
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no other cause of suffering, like, as if craving is sometimes suffering or something.

102
00:08:44,790 --> 00:08:44,950
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

103
00:08:45,110 --> 00:08:47,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever there is suffering, it's because the discrepancy caused by craving.

104
00:08:47,751 --> 00:08:50,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing else.

105
00:08:50,913 --> 00:08:53,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like more or less or a bit later.

106
00:08:53,534 --> 00:08:53,694
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

107
00:08:53,934 --> 00:08:56,555
[SPEAKER_00]: To the extent that there's a discrepancy, to that extent there is suffering.

108
00:08:56,595 --> 00:08:58,055
[SPEAKER_00]: And that discrepancy is what craving is.

109
00:08:59,776 --> 00:09:04,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Either trying to prolong that which doesn't last as long, trying to get rid of that which lasts too long,

110
00:09:07,071 --> 00:09:10,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's certain techniques that some people might do.

111
00:09:10,452 --> 00:09:18,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if someone's, eh... you know, someone who's really an insulting person, who's got a lot of ill will towards you.

112
00:09:18,735 --> 00:09:19,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right.

113
00:09:19,655 --> 00:09:24,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you, when you see them, you sort of think that they're only human, they make mistakes.

114
00:09:24,336 --> 00:09:25,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

115
00:09:25,377 --> 00:09:28,598
[SPEAKER_00]: No, those, all of those things are management of the problem.

116
00:09:28,998 --> 00:09:31,999
[SPEAKER_00]: None of them, management cannot uproot, uproot the problem.

117
00:09:32,259 --> 00:09:34,660
[SPEAKER_00]: It can only manage it, which means it requires it.

118
00:09:35,860 --> 00:09:36,060
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

119
00:09:36,702 --> 00:09:40,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, in the beginning, when there is absolutely... you don't really... like confusion, you don't know what to do.

120
00:09:41,264 --> 00:09:43,824
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, yeah, management can offer some sort of help.

121
00:09:44,405 --> 00:09:53,548
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's a mistake to regard management as a... as a way of practicing towards uprooting the problem altogether, so it won't arise ever again.

122
00:09:54,408 --> 00:09:56,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Because management just covers up, just deals with it.

123
00:09:57,149 --> 00:09:59,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, when the next one comes, you have to manage.

124
00:09:59,030 --> 00:10:00,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, that's not freedom.

125
00:10:01,330 --> 00:10:06,052
[SPEAKER_00]: When you need to do something, whenever this... this occurs, I need to go through these motions, I need to do it.

126
00:10:06,747 --> 00:10:08,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Sooner or later, it's gonna get too much.

127
00:10:09,069 --> 00:10:11,251
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a definition of a burden, duty.

128
00:10:12,653 --> 00:10:21,681
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to aim at the development of mind or meditation and practice, whereby you don't have to do anything on account of suffering, except see it.

129
00:10:22,522 --> 00:10:25,925
[SPEAKER_00]: And you cannot not see it, if you're just mindful.

130
00:10:26,545 --> 00:10:27,947
[SPEAKER_00]: You always see it, because you're mindful.

131
00:10:29,262 --> 00:10:34,464
[SPEAKER_00]: And suffering is something that universally you cannot be oblivious to.

132
00:10:34,684 --> 00:10:40,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, all you have to do is remain mindful, and see the suffering, and then the suffering will cease.

133
00:10:40,746 --> 00:10:46,828
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, if you're doing things rightly, then seeing suffering means uprooting the suffering, because you understood it.

134
00:10:47,728 --> 00:10:50,749
[SPEAKER_00]: But management of suffering does not mean uprooting of suffering.

135
00:10:51,830 --> 00:10:55,391
[SPEAKER_00]: So, management of annoyance does not make you

136
00:10:57,166 --> 00:10:58,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Impenetrable to future are no ends.

137
00:10:58,967 --> 00:10:59,607
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know that.

138
00:10:59,687 --> 00:11:05,510
[SPEAKER_00]: You might become very skilled manager and be able to deal with these things very quickly.

139
00:11:05,830 --> 00:11:09,792
[SPEAKER_00]: But still, you need to deal with it, which means you're affected in the first place.

140
00:11:10,312 --> 00:11:14,314
[SPEAKER_00]: And freedom of it, freedom from it, is when you're not affected, even in the first place.

141
00:11:15,754 --> 00:11:16,635
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the difference.

142
00:11:17,255 --> 00:11:23,298
[SPEAKER_00]: When it comes to freeing yourself from suffering, don't confuse management of it as the way out of it, ultimately.

143
00:11:23,518 --> 00:11:23,898
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not.

144
00:11:25,480 --> 00:11:30,962
[SPEAKER_00]: The way out of it is understanding the nature of it, whereby having suffering arisen means you're free from it.

145
00:11:31,423 --> 00:11:36,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, simply put, having suffering arisen means it can't touch you anymore.

146
00:11:36,085 --> 00:11:46,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Arising of suffering just reminds you that you're free of suffering, which means it's not your problem, which means it's not your suffering, which means there is no suffering anymore.

